title: b4.1 Mindful Walk
syntax: xwiki/2.1
%%WIKI-SYNC-META%%
**Abstract.** Eleven measures. System behaviour: M-01. Walking: M-02 (how much), M-03 (how well remembered). Resident state: M-04 affect, M-05 engagement, M-06 agitation. Resident experience: M-07 autonomy, M-08 privacy and tone. Care and company: M-09 staff time, M-10 human company. Listening: M-11 uptake. Validated instruments where they exist; bespoke counts and codes elsewhere, marked as such.

= Measure - Guide performance [M-01] =

**Report Format:** §1.2 Human Values and Human Factors

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|M-01
|Name|Guide performance
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|Bespoke. Staged-event protocol, as used for prompting systems (Mihailidis et al., 2008).
|Construct measured|Whether the System does what F1 to F6 specify, on time.
|Type|Quantitative
|Instrument or procedure|Staged walks and sessions against a timestamped ground-truth log (EM-01): stops, landmark passes, gate passes, help presses, fall-like drops (device only, onto a mat), no-response periods, refusals, evening requests for news.
|Scoring or coding scheme|Per event: detected (yes/no); latency (s). False triggers per hour. Alert delivery latency (s). Pass thresholds: Open item O-3.
|Validity and reliability|Bespoke. Rests on the accuracy of the ground-truth log.
|Serves|Verification
|Burden|No human subjects. Garden with beacons, prototype, experimenter.

== Graph edges ==

operationalizes → UC01-F1-PR1, UC01-F2-PR2, UC02-F3-PR3, UC01-F4-PR4, UC03-F6-PR6; used-by → EM-01; operationalizes → HV-04.

= Measure - Walking: minutes and completion [M-02] =

**Report Format:** §1.2 Human Values and Human Factors

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|M-02
|Name|Walking: minutes and completion
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|Bespoke count. Walking volume is a standard outcome in exercise studies (Forbes et al., 2015).
|Construct measured|How much the resident walks.
|Type|Quantitative
|Instrument or procedure|Session record (F5) plus companion log. Baseline from care record and observer.
|Scoring or coding scheme|Walks started per week; minutes per walk; share of walks ended early (turned back before the planned end). Against the resident's own baseline.
|Validity and reliability|Counts. Companion log checked against an observer on 20% of walks; ICC ≥ .60.
|Serves|Validation
|Burden|Low. One log line per walk.

== Graph edges ==

operationalizes → UC01-F1-CL1, UC01-F2-CL2, UC01-F2-CL8; used-by → EM-02; operationalizes → HV-05. Associated with HFC-01.

= Measure - Same-day recall [M-03] =

**Report Format:** §1.2 Human Values and Human Factors

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|M-03
|Name|Same-day recall
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|Bespoke. Cued recall and recognition, adapted from memory research. Tests HFC-01.
|Construct measured|Whether events of a walk are recalled better than events of a seated session.
|Type|Quantitative
|Instrument or procedure|(((
Matched events: one distinctive event per walk and per seated session - a bell at the pond or a bell by the window; a named flower or a named object.

One hour later: an open question ("What was there outside today?"), then a choice of three pictures.
)))
|Scoring or coding scheme|Free recall 0/1; recognition 0/1; per event type. Walk minus session, within resident.
|Validity and reliability|Bespoke; no prior validity. Floor effects likely in moderate dementia. Event salience must be matched across conditions.
|Serves|Validation
|Burden|Human subjects. It is a memory test, which IDP-02 avoids: asked as conversation, stopped at any discomfort, in research sessions only.

== Graph edges ==

operationalizes → UC01-F2-CL3; used-by → EM-02. Associated with HFC-01.

= Measure - Observed affect [M-04] =

**Report Format:** §1.2 Human Values and Human Factors

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|M-04
|Name|Observed affect
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|Observed Emotion Rating Scale (Lawton, Van Haitsma & Klapper, 1996), built for residents with Alzheimer's disease. Validated, not invented.
|Construct measured|Pleasure, anxiety, sadness, anger, alertness during the activity.
|Type|Quantitative (observational)
|Instrument or procedure|OERS, rated by a trained observer on the walk or at the session.
|Scoring or coding scheme|Each affect rated for duration in a 10-minute window, on the scale of Lawton et al. (1996). Against baseline.
|Validity and reliability|Psychometrics in Lawton et al. (1996). Less reliable when rated by care assistants than by research staff (Lawton et al., 1999).
|Serves|Validation
|Burden|Human subjects; observer walks along.

== Graph edges ==

operationalizes → UC01-F2-CL2, UC01-F2-CL4, UC01-F2-CL5, UC01-F2-CL6, UC02-F3-CL9, UC03-F6-CL13; used-by → EM-02, EM-04; operationalizes → HV-05.

= Measure - Engagement [M-05] =

**Report Format:** §1.2 Human Values and Human Factors

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|M-05
|Name|Engagement
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|Observational Measurement of Engagement (Cohen-Mansfield, Dakheel-Ali & Marx, 2009), developed with nursing-home residents with dementia.
|Construct measured|Attention to, attitude toward, and time spent with the activity or cue.
|Type|Quantitative (observational)
|Instrument or procedure|OME, per walk segment or session segment.
|Scoring or coding scheme|Duration of engagement; attention and attitude ratings, as defined in the source. Guided against baseline.
|Validity and reliability|Reliability reported in the source. Our segmenting of walks is bespoke; two observers on 20% of segments, ICC ≥ .60.
|Serves|Validation
|Burden|Observer present.

== Graph edges ==

operationalizes → UC01-F2-CL2, UC01-F2-CL4, UC02-F3-CL9; used-by → EM-02, EM-04; operationalizes → HV-05, HV-07.

= Measure - Agitation [M-06] =

**Report Format:** §1.2 Human Values and Human Factors

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|M-06
|Name|Agitation
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|Cohen-Mansfield Agitation Inventory (Cohen-Mansfield, Marx & Rosenthal, 1989). Widely used in nursing homes. Validated, not invented.
|Construct measured|Frequency of agitated behaviour, late afternoon and evening in particular.
|Type|Quantitative (staff-rated)
|Instrument or procedure|CMAI, 29 behaviours, rated by staff over two weeks. Secondary: PRN doses from the care record.
|Scoring or coding scheme|Each behaviour rated 1 (never) to 7 (several times an hour); summed. Phases compared.
|Validity and reliability|Established instrument. Raters trained; same raters across phases.
|Serves|Validation
|Burden|Staff rating time. No extra burden for the resident.

== Graph edges ==

operationalizes → UC02-F3-CL10, UC03-F6-CL13; used-by → EM-02; operationalizes → HV-05.

= Measure - Perceived autonomy [M-07] =

**Report Format:** §1.2 Human Values and Human Factors

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|M-07
|Name|Perceived autonomy
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|Episode-specific need-fulfilment items, with observer counterpart (Custers et al., 2013). Dutch nursing homes; frail residents, not specifically dementia.
|Construct measured|Felt choice and control over the walk, the practice or the listening.
|Type|Quantitative, with observer counterpart
|Instrument or procedure|Three autonomy items after the session, 5-point scale. Observer: 7-point "support of autonomy" scale for the same episode.
|Scoring or coding scheme|Self-report: mean of three items. Observer: separate. Never combined.
|Validity and reliability|α = .69 in the source. Residents rate higher than observers: the observer score carries the Claim.
|Serves|Validation
|Burden|Short interview; only if willing and not distressed.

== Graph edges ==

operationalizes → UC01-F1-CL1, UC03-F6-CL14; used-by → EM-02; operationalizes → HV-01.

= Measure - Perceived privacy and tone [M-08] =

**Report Format:** §1.2 Human Values and Human Factors

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|M-08
|Name|Perceived privacy and tone
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|Bespoke. Code set drawn from Niemeijer et al. (2010, 2015), Kitwood (1997) and Williams et al. (2009).
|Construct measured|Whether residents feel watched, recorded, talked down to, or kept from the news. Whether staff and family judge the record and profile proportionate.
|Type|Mixed: qualitative coding plus a content check
|Instrument or procedure|(((
Residents (EM-02): three open questions after a session - how it was to walk or sit with the voice; whether it felt like being watched; how it spoke to you.

Staff and family (EM-03): interview, including what record and profile hold.

Content check (EM-01): session record and profile against their specification.
)))
|Scoring or coding scheme|(((
Codes: W watched; R recorded or reported on; T talked down to; K kept from news or information; N neutral or positive. A statement can carry more than one code.

Record and profile items named by staff or family: P proportionate, I intrusive.

Content check: pass if exactly the specified items.
)))
|Validity and reliability|Bespoke. Two coders, all interviews; κ ≥ .60 (Landis & Koch, 1977).
|Serves|Both: Verification (content) and Validation (experience)
|Burden|Human subjects for the interviews. Content check: none.

== Graph edges ==

operationalizes → UC01-F2-CL6, UC01-F4-CL7, UC02-F3-CL11, UC03-F6-CL14, UC01-F5-PR5, UC03-F6-PR6; used-by → EM-01, EM-02, EM-03, EM-04; operationalizes → HV-02, HV-03.

= Measure - Care-worker time [M-09] =

**Report Format:** §1.2 Human Values and Human Factors

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|M-09
|Name|Care-worker time
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|Time-sampled observation, method as in Rogers et al. (1999). Our coding is bespoke.
|Construct measured|Staff minutes per walk and per settling episode; calls received.
|Type|Quantitative
|Instrument or procedure|Observer or staff time log.
|Scoring or coding scheme|Minutes per walk, preparation included; minutes per settling episode; calls per day. Against baseline.
|Validity and reliability|Two observers on 20% of episodes; ICC ≥ .60.
|Serves|Validation
|Burden|Staff observed at work: a workplace-surveillance concern (HV-03).

== Graph edges ==

operationalizes → UC01-F2-CL8; used-by → EM-02, EM-03; operationalizes → HV-06.

= Measure - Human company [M-10] =

**Report Format:** §1.2 Human Values and Human Factors

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|M-10
|Name|Human company
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|Bespoke count. Safeguard for VT-07.
|Construct measured|Time the resident spends walking or talking with a person.
|Type|Quantitative
|Instrument or procedure|Activity log: minutes with staff, volunteer, family or walking partner, per week.
|Scoring or coding scheme|Minutes per week, against baseline. Must not fall.
|Validity and reliability|Bespoke. Log completeness checked against observation on sample days.
|Serves|Validation
|Burden|Low. Staff logging.

== Graph edges ==

operationalizes → UC01-F2-CL8; used-by → EM-02, EM-03; operationalizes → HV-07.

= Measure - Listening uptake [M-11] =

**Report Format:** §1.2 Human Values and Human Factors

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|M-11
|Name|Listening uptake
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|Bespoke. Acceptance counts, as commonly used to evaluate recommenders.
|Construct measured|Whether offered content is chosen and kept on.
|Type|Quantitative
|Instrument or procedure|Per offer: accepted, declined, or other choice; minutes listened; stopped early. Baseline: same counts for the default station, observed on sample days.
|Scoring or coding scheme|Acceptance rate; median minutes; early stops.
|Validity and reliability|Counts. Listening is not liking: read alongside M-04.
|Serves|Validation
|Burden|Low. Counted during the study only; not kept in the profile (VT-06).

== Graph edges ==

operationalizes → UC03-F6-CL12; used-by → EM-02; operationalizes → HV-01, HV-08.

= References =

//Cited from memory; checked in the evidence pass.//

* Cohen-Mansfield, J., Marx, M. S. & Rosenthal, A. S. (1989). A description of agitation in a nursing home. //Journal of Gerontology//, 44(3), M77-M84.
* Cohen-Mansfield, J., Dakheel-Ali, M. & Marx, M. S. (2009). Engagement in persons with dementia: the concept and its measurement. //American Journal of Geriatric Psychiatry//, 17(4), 299-307.
* Custers, A. F. J. et al. (2013). Need fulfillment in the nursing home: resident and observer perspectives in relation to resident well-being. //European Journal of Ageing//, 10(3), 201-209.
* Forbes, D. et al. (2015). Exercise programs for people with dementia. //Cochrane Database of Systematic Reviews//, (4), CD006489.
* Landis, J. R. & Koch, G. G. (1977). The measurement of observer agreement for categorical data. //Biometrics//, 33(1), 159-174.
* Lawton, M. P., Van Haitsma, K. & Klapper, J. (1996). Observed affect in nursing home residents with Alzheimer's disease. //Journal of Gerontology B//, 51(1), P3-P14.
* Lawton, M. P. et al. (1999). Observed affect and quality of life in dementia: further affirmations and problems. //Journal of Mental Health and Aging//, 5(1), 69-81.
* Mihailidis, A. et al. (2008). The COACH prompting system to assist older adults with dementia through handwashing: an efficacy study. //BMC Geriatrics//, 8, 28.
* Niemeijer, A. R. et al. (2010, 2015). See b3.1 References.
* Rogers, J. C. et al. (1999). Improving morning care routines of nursing home residents with dementia. //Journal of the American Geriatrics Society//, 47(9), 1049-1057.
* Williams, K. N. et al. (2009). Elderspeak communication: impact on dementia care. //American Journal of Alzheimer's Disease & Other Dementias//, 24(1), 11-20.
